remembering a fallen metro police  officer 49-year-old officer Chong Tai  was responding to a domestic disturbance  call on Thursday morning when he was  shot and killed tonight hundreds came  together for Candlelight vigil to honor  him Madison Kimbrough was there and  spoke with those who knew officer Ty  best she joins us live in The Newsroom  with more Madison officer Tai had been  with Metro for over 20 years and by  tonight's turnout it was clear he  impacted so many lives as a mentor on  the force and a coach in the community  I've been overwhelmed and flooded with  the motions  over the past three years I've had the  pleasure of getting to know  tie better than most it was a solemn  night at Sunset Park as friends family  and fellow law enforcement from across  the valley came together in a  candlelight vigil remembering 49 year  old officer trong Tai who died Thursday  morning while on duty responding to a  domestic disturbance call does he like  mentoring some of these young office  his fellow South Central Area command  officers taking the stage to talk about  his presence on the force Myron ham with  the Las Vegas Police Protection  Association arrived early to help out  and shared with us the type of  individual Ty was having worked with him  for over 20 years I don't think Ty ever  met a person he always met a friend and  that's what that's what I I hope people  remember about him the Asian Community  Development Council and the injured  police officers fund hosted the  candlelight vigil as Ty's family who sat  front row shared pictures with the crowd  many flew in from various parts of the  country to attend sharing with us how  important coaching girls volleyball was  to him and what type of man Ty was  outside of the uniform he definitely  influenced so many decisions and my life  in countless ways Tai was that kind of  person that would encourage you to do  the things that you didn't think that  you had it and need to do on your own  and Governor Steve sisilak and Sheriff  Joe Lombardo were also in attendance  tonight sitting near Ty's family the  candlelight vigil lasted for a little  over an hour in The Newsroom Madison  Kimbrough 8 News Now  and on Monday the injured police  officer's fund is hosting a fundraiser  to help officer Ty's family it starts at  nine at the Nevada Coin Mart near  Flamingo and Jones law enforcement  merchandise will be on sale all proceeds  will go to the family
 

